Kennedy's vote from time to time. It's just got a different feel to it. And the stakes may be thought to be higher in some ways. So I agree that it's got a different tone. That said, you know, Justice Scalia in dissent could write some pretty sharp things. That's right. So do you have a sense that, you know, there's a lot of discussion about what happens after Trump and further discussion about it with a Democratic president, change the composition of the court?
肯尼迪（Kennedy）的投票时的情况有所不同。它的感觉就是不一样。在某些方面，利害关系可能被认为更高。所以我同意它的基调有所不同。话虽如此，你知道的，斯卡利亚（Scalia）大法官在发表异议时确实能写出一些相当犀利的言辞。没错。那么你是否有这样一种感觉，你知道的，有很多关于特朗普之后会发生什么以及与民主党总统进一步讨论的讨论，会改变法院的构成吗？

71
00:42:58.000 --> 00:43:45.000
Do you think it's as simple as that, that, you know, changing the majority could sort of undo some of what's been done during the Roberts era, or are we headed in a totally different direction? I think a change in personnel would, in short order, move the court in a different direction, jurisprudentially. There was a moment in time, you'll remember, when it looked like President Obama would be able to replace Justice Scalia with Merrick Garland. He didn't get a hearing in the end. But political scientists predicted that if that happened, cases like Citizens United and Heller on the Second Amendment could well be reversed.
你认为事情就这么简单吗，你知道的，改变多数派可以某种程度上撤销罗伯茨时代所做的一些事情，还是我们正朝着一个完全不同的方向前进？我认为人员的变动会在短时间内在法理学层面上将法院推向一个不同的方向。你会记得，曾几何时，看起来奥巴马总统将能够用梅里克·加兰（Merrick Garland）取代斯卡利亚大法官。他最终没有得到听证会。但政治学家预测，如果发生这种情况，像《联合公民案》（Citizens United）和关于第二修正案的《海勒案》（Heller）等判决很可能会被推翻。

72
00:43:45.000 --> 00:44:20.000
It's entirely conceivable. I mean, stare decisis is a game two sides can play. But at great risk of really undermining the court's legitimacy, don't you think? I guess, although, you know, we talk about the court's legitimacy, and the court has been quite active. And its approval ratings have fallen some. but in truth, they're still higher than most of the rest of the government. The military is higher, but Congress is in the toilet. It's higher than journalists. Probably higher than the academy.
这完全是可以想象的。我的意思是，遵循先例（stare decisis）是双方都可以玩的游戏。但在极大地威胁法院合法性的巨大风险下，你不这么认为吗？我猜，尽管，你知道的，我们讨论法院的合法性，而且法院一直相当活跃。其支持率有所下降。但在现实中，它们仍然高于政府的大多数其他部门。军方的支持率更高，但国会简直烂透了（in the toilet）。它比记者高。可能比学术界还要高。

73
00:44:20.000 --> 00:45:09.000
Right, for sure. And I mean, at the end of the day, I think all legitimacy means is, will people do what the court says? And so far, there's no indication that people won't. Do you think that the idea of legitimacy, which I only hear in reference to the judicial branch, or the idea of reason and logic being the ruling principle, is that a consequence of the vagueness of Article 3 that's built on top of kind of we're building our foundation as it during the creation of the thing, you know, in a way? So it has to have some other sort of foundation to stand on. I think that's a good point. And a little too abstract for the likes of my simple-mindedness.
对，当然。我的意思是，归根结底，我认为合法性的全部意义就在于：人们会听从法院的话吗？到目前为止，没有任何迹象表明人们不会。你认为合法性的概念——我只在提及司法部门时听到过——或者理性与逻辑是统治原则的概念，是《宪法第三条》模糊性的一种后果，这种模糊性建立在我们边创建事物边奠定基础的过程中，你知道的，某种程度上？因此它必须有其他某种基础来立足。我认为这是一个很好的观点。而对于我这种思维简单的人来说，这有点太抽象了。

74
00:45:09.000 --> 00:45:54.000
Fair enough. Well, you know, one thing to think about with legitimacy, though, is I think there's a, you know, there's a tension here with the courts have to be legitimate. That's part of their independence. At the same time, I think a lot of individual, some individual federal judges feel under threat or under attack. And I want to go back for a moment to Chief Justice Roberts, who said, I believe that is 2024. end of the year report, he openly acknowledged that, look, you can't, you can't threaten the judiciary. That is unacceptable, right? And then this just past year's report, he had a kind of rosy-colored vision of the country's 250th birthday. And I just, it's such a swing back and forth. Where do you see
这倒也是。好吧，你知道，关于合法性，有一件事需要思考，那就是我认为这里存在着一种紧张关系，法院必须具有合法性。这是他们独立性的一部分。与此同时，我认为许多个人，一些联邦法官感到自己受到了威胁或攻击。我想回过头来谈谈罗伯茨首席大法官，我相信那是2024年的年底报告，他公开承认，看，你不能，你不能威胁司法部门。那是不可接受的，对吧？然后就在去年的报告中，他对国家成立250周年抱有一种玫瑰色的愿景。我只是觉得，这种前后摇摆太大了。你认为

75
00:45:54.000 --> 00:46:26.000
your sense of how the judiciary is responding, sometimes to literal threats, sometimes to social media threats of impeachment, things of that nature? I don't know what to say other than that these threats are real. The judiciary is quite concerned about them. The Supreme Court has excellent security. Lower court judges, much less so. They're deeply concerned about it. I think the Chief Justice should speak out about it often and robustly.
司法部门的反应如何，有时是对字面上的威胁的反应，有时是对社交媒体上弹劾威胁等性质事情的反应？除了这些威胁是真实存在的之外，我不知道还能说些什么。司法部门对此非常关切。最高法院拥有极佳的安保。下级法院的法官则远没有那么好。他们对此深感担忧。我认为首席大法官应该经常且有力地对此大声疾呼。

76
00:46:26.000 --> 00:47:01.000
I don't think it's affecting their work, but I don't know. it's different in kind from anything I've lived through before. Political violence is, I guess it's always been with us, but it seems to be woven into the texture of ordinary American life these days in a way that's really disturbing. More with the New York Times, Adam Liptack. After the break. If there's something that, you know, you would change,
我不认为这正在影响他们的工作，但我不知道。这与我以前经历过的任何事情在本质上都不同。政治暴力，我猜它一直伴随着我们，但它现在似乎已经编织进了普通美国人生活的纹理中，这真的很令人不安。更多内容请关注《纽约时报》的亚当·利普塔克（Adam Liptack）。广告之后继续。如果有什么事情，你知道的，你会去改变的话，

77
00:47:01.000 --> 00:47:57.000
what do you think is the right way to make things better? I know you're a reporter and your, You're just trying to tell the story of what happened. But as you see it close up, what do you think would be like an effective way to make the Supreme Court more effective or the decisions make more sense? You know, reporters always want transparency. Yeah. I would love to know, for instance, the vote counts on these emergency applications we were talking about earlier or when the court decides not to hear a case, how many justices voted for that or not. They also have a tendency. It's not the world's hardest working court. I mean, they issue maybe 60 decisions on the merits a year. But they do tend to do everything all at the same time. So there are days when they agree to hear cases and issue opinions and hear arguments and then they go away for two weeks.
你认为让事情变得更好的正确方式是什么？我知道你是一名记者，你的，你只是想讲述发生了什么的故事。但从你近距离的观察来看，你认为能让最高法院更有效率或者让判决更有意义的有效方法会是什么？你知道，记者总是追求透明度。是的。比如，我很想知道我们之前讨论过的这些紧急申请的投票统计数据，或者当法院决定不审理某个案件时，有多少大法官投了赞成票或反对票。他们也有这样一种倾向。这并不是世界上最勤奋的法院。我的意思是，他们每年大概只对实体问题做出大约60项判决。但他们确实往往把所有事情都在同一时间堆在一起。所以有些日子里，他们同意审理案件、发布意见书、听取辩论，然后他们就离开两周。

78
00:47:57.000 --> 00:48:53.000
If they were to space those things out a little bit, that would be nice. Do you think it would be a good idea if the Supreme Court arguments were televised live? I'm in favor of that. It's an academic question only. It's not going to happen. I don't think it would add a whole lot. You know, it's an open government proceeding. It ought to be open to the public in the realest sense imaginable. That said, the live audio, which is an artifact of the pandemic and would have been unimaginable 10 years ago, gets you 95% of the way. Certainly if you can recognize the justice as voices. You're really getting almost all the information that you need. I think some of the justices value their privacy, although their book tours would seem to cut in the other direction. But that's not a legitimate reason, not to have cameras. Some of them say goofy things on the bench,
如果他们能把这些事情稍微间隔开一点，那就好了。你认为最高法院的辩论进行电视直播会是一个好主意吗？我对此表示赞成。这纯粹是一个学术问题。这不会发生。我不认为这会增加多少内容。你知道的，这是一场公开的政府诉讼。它本应以你能想象得到的、最真实意义上的方式向公众开放。话虽如此，现场音频（这是大流行的产物，在10年前是不可想象的）已经能带你达到95%的效果。当然，如果你能认出大法官的声音的话。你实际上得到了你所需几乎所有的信息。我认为一些大法官很珍视他们的隐私，尽管他们的图书巡回宣传似乎指向相反的方向。但这不是不安装摄像头的正当理由。他们中的一些人在法庭上说一些愚蠢的话，

79
00:48:53.000 --> 00:49:39.000
and they're afraid that that would turn up on late-night television. That's an authentic concern, but not a legitimate one. Are there security concerns around this? I don't know. But anyway, I mean, this is a thought exercise only. They're not going to do it. So putting its constitutionally aside, one thought I'd had is, you know, would it be better for the system if the justices were just told, look, when you hear argument, you have to issue an opinion, you know, let's say a month later. And the reason I raise this is because, you know, as you say, Trump versus the United States, the presidential immunity opinions, maybe the most significant decision of the court so far. and they waited. They waited until the very, very end,
他们担心这些话会出现在深夜电视节目中。这是一个真实的担忧，但不是一个正当的担忧。这周围是否存在安全隐患？我不知道。但无论如何，我的意思是，这只是一种思想实验。他们不会这么做的。所以撇开宪法不谈，我曾有过的一个想法是，你知道的，如果告诉大法官们：看，当你听取辩论时，你必须在一个月左右后发表意见，这对体制会不会更好？我提出这一点的原因是，你知道的，正如你所说，特朗普诉美国案（Trump versus the United States）、总统豁免权意见，可能是迄今为止法院最重要的判决。而他们等了。他们一直等到非常、非常最后的时候，

80
00:49:39.000 --> 00:50:03.000
and that timing made it impossible for anything really to go on in the case at all. And, you know, that was presumably deliberate or they couldn't come to an agreement on things. I'd love to hear your thoughts on that. So I think the idea of making them work fast as a general proposition is a bad idea. They don't do good work working fast.
而这个时机使得该案根本不可能再进行任何实质性的审理。而且，你知道的，这大概是故意的，或者他们在某些问题上无法达成一致。我很想听听你对此的想法。所以，我认为普遍要求他们快速工作的想法是个坏想法。他们在快速工作时做不出好作品。

81
00:50:03.000 --> 00:50:42.000
And I actually give them some credit, and not every court is like this, disposing of every merits case, almost without exception, within the October to June term. What they might have done in Trump against the United States is, you remember, Jack Smith went to the court twice, once a procedure called cert before judgment seeking to leapfrog the D.C. Circuit. I think back in October. Very early. And they turned that down. And if they had taken the case then rather than later,
而且我实际上对他们给予了一些肯定，并非每个法院都像这样，他们几乎无一例外地在10月至6月的开庭期内处理了每一个实体案件。他们在《特朗普诉美国案》中可能做过的是，你还记得，杰克·史密斯（Jack Smith）曾两次找最高法院，一次是通过一种名为“判决前移审令”（cert before judgment）的程序，试图跳过华盛顿特区巡回上诉法院。我想是在10月份。非常早。而他们拒绝了。如果他们当时而不是后来接手这个案件，

82
00:50:42.000 --> 00:51:22.000
they might have had some running room to issue a decision and maybe a more considered decision. April arguments almost always yield pretty poor decisions. It's just not enough time for them. And they've already backed up on everything else. So April arguments and June decisions don't tend to be at the court's best work. Hmm. Interesting. Is there anything else sort of, you know, from your vantage point, the way the court works that is interesting or surprising to you as you've been covering this for so long? You know, I'm so close to it. I probably don't realize what I should, which should be interesting or surprising.
他们可能就会有一些腾挪的空间来发布判决，也许是一个经过更多深思熟虑的判决。4月份的辩论几乎总是产生相当糟糕的判决。对他们来说时间实在是不够。而且他们已经在所有其他事情上都积压了。所以4月份的辩论和6月份的判决往往不是法院的最佳水准。嗯。有趣。从你的角度来看，在你报道了这么长时间之后，法院运作的方式中还有没有什么其他让你觉得有趣或惊讶的事情？你知道的，我离它太近了。我可能没有意识到什么本该是有趣或惊奇的事情。

83
00:51:22.000 --> 00:52:08.000
I guess I should say that I kind of stepped back from Daily Beat reporting at the end of this last. term. So I have, I'm still writing about the court, but not on this kind of daily ruling basis. My colleagues, Ann Merrimow and Abby Van Sickle have taken over the hardest part of the job. And I'm more of an analytical observer. So I'm, I'm kind of decompressing from 17 years of Supreme Court charge. Like, would people be surprised by how much, like, clerks do versus justice is and things like this. The justices each have four Brainiac law clerks.
我想我应该说，我在上一个开庭期结束时，从每日突发新闻报道（Daily Beat reporting）中退居二线了。所以我现在，我仍在撰写关于法院的文章，但不是以这种日常裁决为基础的。我的同事安·麦里莫（Ann Marimow）和艾比·范西克尔（Abby Van Sickle）接手了这项工作中最高难度部分。而我更像是一个分析观察家。所以我在，我正在从17年的最高法院报道生涯中放松下来。比如，人们会对法官和法官助理（clerks）各自做了多少工作感到惊讶吗，诸如此类的事情。每位大法官都有四名聪明绝顶的法律助理。

84
00:52:08.000 --> 00:52:26.000
Who work around the clock. Yeah. Well, maybe we could pause for a moment. Maybe we could just explain. What do you mean by a clerk? Maybe readers will think, oh, someone who writes down appointments for the justices. What do you mean by a clerk? So these clerkships are one year long.
他们日夜工作。嗯，也许我们可以暂停一下。也许我们可以简单解释一下。你所说的助理（clerk）是什么意思？也许读者会想，哦，就是给大法官记预约的人。你所说的助理是什么意思？所以这些助理职位为期一年。

85
00:52:26.000 --> 00:53:05.000
They are given to young people who are law school graduates and who have typically clerked for one, now sometimes two lower court judges. They will have the most distinguished resumes imaginable and excellent grades and great skills, but maybe not yet great maturity. And they are given extraordinary responsibilities, including in evaluating the petition seeking review, which are sent out to the so-called cert pool where a single 26-year-old makes a preliminary judgment for the Supreme Court of the United States
它们授予那些法学院毕业的年轻人，他们通常担任过一名，现在有时是两名下级法院法官的助理。他们拥有你能想象到的最杰出的简历、优秀的成绩和出色的技能，但可能还没有足够的高度成熟。他们被赋予了非凡的职责，包括评估寻求审查的请愿书，这些请愿书被发送到所谓的审理池（cert pool），在那里，一个26岁的年轻人为美国最高法院做出初步判断，

86
00:53:05.000 --> 00:53:38.000
about whether to grant review or not. And, you know, these clerks make up a little law firm with the justice at its head. And as in a real law firm, the associates write the first draft of a brief or an opinion. And the justice is directed and, you know, are responsible for it. And most of them write through it fairly carefully.
决定是否批准审查。而且，你知道的，这些助理与以大法官为首的法官构成了一个小型律师事务所。正如在真正的律师事务所里一样，初级律师（associates）撰写简报或判决书的第一稿。而大法官则进行指导，你知道的，并对此负责。他们大多数人都相当仔细地通读并修改了这些稿件。

87
00:53:38.000 --> 00:54:06.000
But the clerk has a substantial hand in what happens. Not at all contemplated by Article III. We'll search in vain for law clerk in the Constitution. Yes, it is a remarkable aspect of the, judiciary that, you know, it's not, what you're mentioning is, of course, the most important clerks at the top of the heap on the Supreme Court, but every federal judge has one or more clerks who serve in the same role.
但助理对事情的走向有很大的插手。这根本不在《宪法第三条》的设想之中。你在宪法里翻遍也找不到“法律助理”这个词。是的，这是司法部门的一个显著方面，你知道的，这不是——你所提及的当然是最高法院顶端最重要的助理，但每一个联邦法院法官都有一名或多名担任同样角色的助理。

88
00:54:06.000 --> 00:54:44.000
Well, you know, one thing we haven't talked about is the background of the justices, which is increasingly turning into a kind of inherited aristocracy. Say more about that, please. I mean, the justices themselves have sterling resumes, eight of the nine went to Harvard or Yale, law school, and several of them not only clerked on the Supreme Court. I mean, the odds of that are sort of vanishingly small. There's not that many clerkships to go around, but two of them, three of them, three of them clerked for the very justice whom they succeeded. Right.
好吧，你知道，我们还没谈到的一件事是大法官的背景，这正日益变成一种世袭贵族制。请多谈谈这一点。我的意思是，大法官们本身拥有极佳的简历，九人中有八人毕业于哈佛或耶鲁法学院，其中几人不仅在最高法院担任过助理。我的意思是，这种概率简直微乎其微。没有那么多助理职位可供分配，但其中两人，三人，三他们三人担任过他们所接替的那位大法官的助理。对。

89
00:54:44.000 --> 00:55:40.000
I mean, that's curious, but Roberts for Rehnquist and Jackson for Breyer, and, Kavanaugh for Kennedy? I mean, what are the odds of that? And that ties back a little bit to the point I was making earlier about timing of retirements. I don't have evidence for this, but I think it may be possible that as a justice is deciding whether to retire or not, that justice might have consultations with the White House and say, you know, I might like to retire and it sure be nice if you would appoint my favorite clerk to succeed me. And it's remarkable because, of course, in other aspects of the Constitution, we have, for instance, prohibitions on granting titles of nobility because that very value is something the founders said, we don't want that system. And yet it's crept into the Supreme Court, as you mentioned.
我的意思是，这很奇妙，但罗伯茨是伦奎斯特（Rehnquist）的助理，杰克逊（Jackson）是布雷耶（Breyer）的助理，卡瓦诺是肯尼迪的助理？我的意思是，这种概率有多大？这稍微回溯到了我之前提出的关于退休时机的一点。我没有证据证明这一点，但我认为有这种可能，即当一位大法官在决定是否退休时，这位大法官可能会与白宫进行协商，并说，你知道，我可能想退休，如果你能任命我最喜欢的助理来接替我，那就太好了。这很了不起，因为当然，在宪法的其他方面，我们有，例如，关于禁止授予贵族称号的规定，因为正是这种价值观，奠基人们说过：我们不想要那种体制。然而它却悄悄潜入了最高法院，正如你所提到的。

90
00:55:40.000 --> 00:56:34.000
It is remarkable to me, too, in the sense that like even in the sort of the text itself, the idea of lifetime appointments, in a constitution that is full of things that are basically guards against any type of thing that smacks is. as monarchy. Like in terms of even the age of the president can be, it's kind of a backdoor way of, you know, like George Washington not giving it to a six-year-old kid in the presidency. And this is an odd thing that we've set up this side channel where these titles of nobility are our lifetime and can be, you know, functionally passed on is an oddity. Right. Well, one last thing maybe we should address is, you know, this Article III is very, very short. And strangely enough, in this very short part of the Constitution, there's an entire section voted to treason, which seems to totally be a non-sequitur. It has nothing to do with the preceding part of Article 3.
